'''''Gogia''''' is a [[genus]] of primitive [[Eocrinoidea|eocrinoid]] [[blastozoa]]n from the early to [[middle Cambrian]].

''G. ojenai'' dates to the late Early Cambrian;<ref name=Durham1978/> other [[species]] come from various Middle Cambrian strata throughout North America, but the genus has yet to be described outside this [[continent]].<ref name=Robison1965>{{harvnb|Robison|1965}}</ref>  Notable localities where species are found include the [[Wheeler Shale]] of [[Utah]],<ref>{{harvnb|Schlottke|2007}}</ref> and the [[Burgess Shale]] of [[British Columbia]].{{citation needed|date=July 2017}}

The species of ''Gogia'', like other eocrinoids, were not closely related to the true [[crinoid]]s, instead, being more closely related to the [[blastoid]]s.
[[Image:Gogia radiata.jpg|thumb|left|''Gogia radiata'']] 
''Gogia'' is distinguished from sea lilies, and most other blastoids, in that the plate-covered body was shaped like a vase, or a bowling pin (with the pin part stuck into the substrate), and that the five [[ambulacral|ambulacra]] were split into pairs of coiled or straight, ribbon-like strands. Six specimens of ''Gogia'' are known from the Greater [[Phyllopod bed]], where they comprise < 0.1% of the community.<ref name=Caron2006>{{harvnb|Caron|Jackson|2006}}</ref>

As a whole, the Eocrinoids are regarded as basal blastozoans very close to the ancestry of the entire subphylum.

